      "text": "To solve the problem of finding how many odd numbers between 100 and 999 have distinct digits, we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's identify the structure of the numbers we are looking for. An odd number ends in an odd digit. The possible odd digits are 1, 3, 5, 7, and 9. Therefore, the last digit of the number can be one of these five digits.\n\n1. **Choose the last digit:**\n   There are 5 possible choices for the last digit (1, 3, 5, 7, or 9).\n\n2. **Choose the first digit:**\n   The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9, except it cannot be the same as the last digit. So, there are 9 possible choices for the first digit.\n\n3. **Choose the second digit:**\n   The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9, except it must be different from the first and last digits. So, there are 9 possible choices for the second digit (it cannot be the same as the first or last digit).\n\nNow, we can calculate the total number of such numbers by multiplying the number of choices for each digit:\n\\[\n5 \\text{ (choices for the last digit)} \\times 9 \\text{ (choices for the first digit)} \\times 9 \\text{ (choices for the second digit)} = 5 \\times 9 \\times 9 = 405\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 that have distinct digits is \\(\\boxed{405}\\).",
